{%- comment -%}
  Fit, widened into a real garment chart.

  WHAT CHANGED AND WHY. The old table ran four columns, Size / Chest /
  Waist / Length, with the unit typed into every cell ("38 in"). Four
  columns is not enough to buy a waistcoat: a coat that fits the chest
  and misses the shoulder is a coat that goes back, and shoulder is the
  one measurement a tailor cannot let out afterwards. So the set is now
  the six the shop actually cuts to, Size / Chest / Waist / Length /
  Shoulder / Collar, and the unit has moved out of the cells and into a
  toggle, which is both less to type and the only way centimetres can be
  offered without the client maintaining a second chart by hand.

  THE TABLE ITSELF IS NOT IN THIS FILE. It is snippets/lada-size-table,
  because the same markup has to render a second time as a popover
  beside the PDP size selector, which is the moment the question is
  actually asked. Rendering it from two places is the whole point of the
  snippet: edit the chart once, both entry points move.

  THE DRAWING IS DRAWN, NOT SHOT. Every other picture on this page is
  waiting on a photoshoot that has produced exactly one Lada frame so
  far. A measurement diagram has no business waiting for a camera, so it
  is inline SVG at 1px, champagne through currentColor, no fill, no
  file, no request, and it renders correctly the day this ships.

  WHY THE DRAWING CARRIES NUMBERS AND THE NAMES SIT BESIDE IT. Setting
  the words inside the SVG would put six client-editable strings at
  fixed coordinates with no way to reflow: one long label and the
  drawing has type hanging off its edge, which is exactly the kind of
  break a client filling the page himself would never see coming. So the
  garment carries numerals, which cannot overflow, and the numerals are
  keyed to a text list that wraps like text. The SVG is aria-hidden
  because the list already says everything it says.

  THE POINTS ARE BLOCKS, THE ROWS ARE A SETTING. A block per measurement
  point lets the client drop one he does not measure and reorder the
  rest, and the numbering follows the block order in both the drawing
  and the list because both read it from the same loop. Each block picks
  its anchor from a fixed set of six, so the geometry stays correct no
  matter how they are ordered; a free text position would let the label
  and the callout drift apart. Table rows stay a single textarea because
  a row is not a design decision, it is data, and forty cells across six
  blocks would be a worse thing to edit, not a better one.

  BUT AN EMPTY BLOCK LIST IS NOT A CHOICE, IT IS THE DEFAULT, AND THE
  FIRST VERSION OF THIS SECTION READ IT AS A CHOICE. templates/page.lada
  .json already carries this section with three settings and no blocks,
  and Shopify applies a preset only when a section is inserted fresh in
  the editor, never to one already placed in a template. So keying the
  drawing to blocks alone deleted it from the live page: the shop got a
  chart and no diagram, silently, and nothing in the editor said why.
  The anchors are a closed set of six, so no blocks now means the whole
  six, drawn in the order the tape goes round the body. The first point
  block added takes the drawing over completely. Removing the drawing on
  purpose is the checkbox, which is a decision the client can see.

  DEGRADES BOTH WAYS. No JavaScript and the chart is in inches, correct
  and readable, with the dead toggle never revealed. Drawing switched
  off and the figure leaves the DOM and the chart runs full width.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign wa = section.settings.whatsapp | default: '923019090590'
  assign wa_text = section.settings.whatsapp_text | default: 'I need help with Lada sizing. My measurements are:'

  # Only 'point' blocks drive the drawing, so a future block type cannot
  # silently add a numeral to the garment.
  assign points = section.blocks | where: 'type', 'point'

  # See the note above: no blocks is the shipped state, not an
  # instruction, so it falls back to the full six rather than to
  # nothing. use_defaults is what the two loops branch on, because the
  # fallback list is plain strings and a block is an object.
  assign use_defaults = false
  if points.size == 0
    assign use_defaults = true
    assign points = 'collar,shoulder,chest,armhole,waist,length' | split: ','
    assign default_labels = section.settings.default_labels | default: 'Collar,Shoulder,Chest,Armhole,Waist,Length' | split: ','
  endif

  # A checkbox absent from a JSON template still resolves to its schema
  # default, unlike blocks, so this is true on the live page today.
  assign draw = section.settings.show_diagram

  assign fit_class = 'lada__fit'
  unless draw
    assign fit_class = 'lada__fit lada__fit--nodiagram'
  endunless

  assign howto = section.settings.howto | default: 'Measure over a shirt, not over bare skin and not over a coat.|Chest: around the fullest point, tape level under the arms.|Waist: at the navel, with one finger under the tape.|Length: from the base of the collar to where you want the coat to end.' | split: '|'
-%}
<section class="lada" id="lada-sizing" aria-labelledby="lada-sizing-h">
  <div class="lada__wrap lada__sec">
    <p class="lada__kicker">{{ section.settings.kicker | default: 'Fit' }}</p>
    <h2 class="lada__h2" id="lada-sizing-h">{{ section.settings.heading | default: 'Bespoke sizing' }}</h2>
    <p class="lada__p">{{ section.settings.intro | default: 'Waistcoats and prince coats are cut 38R through 46R as standard. Anything outside that is measured in the shop at no extra cost.' }}</p>

    <div class="{{ fit_class }}">

      <div class="lada__fit-table">
        {%- comment -%}
          uid is the section id, so the popover instance on a product
          page can never collide with this one over a radio name.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- render 'lada-size-table',
              uid: section.id,
              rows: section.settings.rows,
              columns: section.settings.columns,
              caption_in: section.settings.caption_in,
              caption_cm: section.settings.caption_cm,
              note: section.settings.table_note,
              units_legend: section.settings.units_legend,
              unit_in_label: section.settings.unit_in_label,
              unit_cm_label: section.settings.unit_cm_label -%}
      </div>

      {%- if draw -%}
        <figure class="lada__diagram">
          {%- comment -%}
            The caption leads the figure. HTML allows figcaption only as
            the first or last child, and it was sitting between the svg
            and the list, which is neither; reading order is better this
            way round too, because the label arrives before the drawing
            it names rather than after it.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <figcaption class="lada__diagram-cap">
            {{ section.settings.diagram_title | default: 'Where we measure' }}
          </figcaption>

          {%- comment -%}
            viewBox 300x320, garment centred on x=150, the two outer
            strips kept clear for callouts. stroke-width is 1 and
            vector-effect:non-scaling-stroke is applied in CSS, so the
            line stays a true hairline at every rendered size instead of
            fattening as the drawing grows.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <svg class="lada__diagram-svg" viewBox="0 0 300 320" width="300" height="320"
               fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"
               stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
               aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">

            {%- comment -%}
              The garment. One closed outline, then the front opening,
              the button run, and two welt pockets. The armholes are
              scooped inward rather than bowed out, which is the single
              line that stops a sleeveless flat reading as an arch.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <path d="M128 32 L92 46 C94 74 80 96 66 120 C72 150 74 172 70 196 L78 244
                     L144 284 L150 274 L156 284 L222 244 L230 196 C226 172 228 150 234 120
                     C220 96 206 74 208 46 L172 32 C164 25 136 25 128 32 Z"/>
            <path d="M128 32 L150 148 L172 32"/>
            <path d="M150 148 L150 274"/>
            <circle cx="150" cy="168" r="3"/>
            <circle cx="150" cy="198" r="3"/>
            <circle cx="150" cy="228" r="3"/>
            <circle cx="150" cy="258" r="3"/>
            <path d="M88 216 L118 210"/>
            <path d="M182 210 L212 216"/>

            {%- comment -%}
              One group per point, in block order, so the numeral on the
              garment and the numeral in the list are the same loop
              index. Each anchor owns its dashed measurement line, its
              leader and its slot, and the slots are placed so that no
              leader crosses another line: three down the left, three
              down the right, with the length dimension outside them all
              at x=278 where nothing has to reach across it.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- for p in points -%}
              {%- liquid
                assign n = forloop.index
                if use_defaults
                  assign anchor = p
                else
                  assign anchor = p.settings.anchor
                endif
              -%}
              {%- case anchor -%}

                {%- when 'collar' -%}
                  <g class="lada__diagram-call">
                    <path d="M132 22 L168 22" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
                    <path d="M132 17 L132 27 M168 17 L168 27"/>
                    <path d="M33 20 L129 22"/>
                    <circle cx="24" cy="20" r="9"/>
                    <text x="24" y="20" dy=".36em" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11"
                          font-weight="600" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">{{ n }}</text>
                  </g>

                {%- when 'shoulder' -%}
                  <g class="lada__diagram-call">
                    <path d="M92 42 L208 42" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
                    <path d="M92 37 L92 47 M208 37 L208 47"/>
                    <path d="M243 22 L211 40"/>
                    <circle cx="250" cy="16" r="9"/>
                    <text x="250" y="16" dy=".36em" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11"
                          font-weight="600" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">{{ n }}</text>
                  </g>

                {%- when 'chest' -%}
                  <g class="lada__diagram-call">
                    <path d="M62 124 L238 124" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
                    <path d="M62 119 L62 129 M238 119 L238 129"/>
                    <path d="M33 124 L58 124"/>
                    <circle cx="24" cy="124" r="9"/>
                    <text x="24" y="124" dy=".36em" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11"
                          font-weight="600" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">{{ n }}</text>
                  </g>

                {%- when 'armhole' -%}
                  <g class="lada__diagram-call">
                    <path d="M202 56 C198 80 210 98 222 118" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
                    <path d="M254 70 L213 53"/>
                    <circle cx="262" cy="64" r="9"/>
                    <text x="262" y="64" dy=".36em" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11"
                          font-weight="600" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">{{ n }}</text>
                  </g>

                {%- when 'waist' -%}
                  <g class="lada__diagram-call">
                    <path d="M62 186 L238 186" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
                    <path d="M62 181 L62 191 M238 181 L238 191"/>
                    <path d="M33 186 L58 186"/>
                    <circle cx="24" cy="186" r="9"/>
                    <text x="24" y="186" dy=".36em" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11"
                          font-weight="600" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">{{ n }}</text>
                  </g>

                {%- when 'length' -%}
                  <g class="lada__diagram-call">
                    <path d="M278 32 L278 280" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
                    <path d="M272 32 L284 32 M272 280 L284 280"/>
                    <path d="M278 293 L278 286"/>
                    <circle cx="278" cy="302" r="9"/>
                    <text x="278" y="302" dy=".36em" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11"
                          font-weight="600" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">{{ n }}</text>
                  </g>

              {%- endcase -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          </svg>

          <ol class="lada__points">
            {%- for p in points -%}
              {%- liquid
                if use_defaults
                  assign anchor = p
                  assign plabel = default_labels[forloop.index0]
                  assign pnote = ''
                else
                  assign anchor = p.settings.anchor
                  assign plabel = p.settings.label
                  assign pnote = p.settings.note
                endif
                # A numeral on the garment with nothing beside it is a
                # riddle, so a cleared name falls back to the anchor.
                if plabel == blank
                  assign plabel = anchor | capitalize
                endif
              -%}
              <li{% unless use_defaults %} {{ p.shopify_attributes }}{% endunless %}>
                <span class="n" aria-hidden="true">{{ forloop.index }}</span>
                <span class="lada__point-t">{{ plabel }}</span>
                {%- if pnote != blank -%}
                  <span class="lada__point-n">{{ pnote }}</span>
                {%- endif -%}
              </li>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </ol>
        </figure>
      {%- endif -%}

      <div class="lada__fit-help">
        {%- comment -%}
          Hairlines, not a filled panel. The table above it and the
          drawing beside it are both drawn in 1px rules on the ground,
          and a bordered tinted box in the middle of them would be the
          only closed shape in the section.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <h3 class="lada__howto-h">{{ section.settings.help_title | default: 'How to measure' }}</h3>
        <ul class="lada__howto">
          {%- comment -%}
            The text is wrapped rather than measured on the li itself:
            the li carries the hairline, and a max-width on it stopped
            every rule 190px short of the chart's rules directly above,
            which is the one thing a section built out of hairlines
            cannot afford.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- for line in howto -%}
            <li><span class="lada__howto-t">{{ line | strip }}</span></li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </ul>

        <div class="lada__fit-act">
          <a class="btn-lada"
             href="https://wa.me/{{ wa }}?text={{ wa_text | url_encode }}"
             target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            {{- section.settings.cta_label | default: 'Send measurements on WhatsApp' -}}</a>
          {%- if section.settings.secondary_label != blank -%}
            <a class="lada__link" href="{{ section.settings.secondary_url | default: '#lada-measure' }}">
              {{- section.settings.secondary_label -}}</a>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Lada sizing",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "text", "id": "kicker", "label": "Kicker", "default": "Fit" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "Bespoke sizing" },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "intro", "label": "Intro" },

    { "type": "header", "content": "The chart" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "columns", "label": "Column headings",
      "info": "Comma separated. The first column is the size; every other column is a measurement. Type them in sentence case, the capitals are added by the theme.",
      "default": "Size,Chest,Waist,Length,Shoulder,Collar" },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "rows", "label": "Rows",
      "info": "One row per line break is not used here: separate rows with a pipe, cells with a comma. Enter inches as plain numbers, no unit, no quotes. Centimetres are worked out by the toggle.",
      "default": "38R,38,32,29,16.5,15|40R,40,34,29.5,17,15.5|42R,42,36,30,17.5,16|44R,44,38,30.5,18,16.5|46R,46,40,31,18.5,17" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "caption_in", "label": "Caption, inches",
      "default": "All measurements in inches, taken on the body." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "caption_cm", "label": "Caption, centimetres",
      "default": "All measurements in centimetres, taken on the body." },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "table_note", "label": "Note under the chart",
      "default": "These are body measurements, not the finished garment. The ease a waistcoat needs is added on the cutting table." },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Unit toggle" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "The chart is written in inches and the toggle converts. It only appears once the theme's script has loaded, so nobody is offered a control that cannot work." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "units_legend", "label": "Group label", "default": "Units" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "unit_in_label", "label": "Inches button", "default": "Inches" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "unit_cm_label", "label": "Centimetres button", "default": "Centimetres" },

    { "type": "header", "content": "How to measure" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "help_title", "label": "Title", "default": "How to measure" },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "howto", "label": "Lines", "info": "Separate with a pipe." },

    { "type": "header", "content": "The drawing" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "The waistcoat is drawn by the theme, so it needs no photograph. With no point blocks it shows all six standard points; add one block and your blocks take over entirely. Untick the box to drop the drawing and run the chart full width." },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_diagram", "label": "Show the drawing", "default": true },
    { "type": "text", "id": "diagram_title", "label": "Caption", "default": "Where we measure" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "default_labels", "label": "Standard point names",
      "info": "Used only until you add point blocks. Comma separated, in this order: collar, shoulder, chest, armhole, waist, length.",
      "default": "Collar,Shoulder,Chest,Armhole,Waist,Length" },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Routes" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "cta_label", "label": "Button", "default": "Send measurements on WhatsApp" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "whatsapp", "label": "WhatsApp number", "default": "923019090590" },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "whatsapp_text", "label": "Pre-filled message" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "secondary_label", "label": "Secondary link", "default": "Or be measured in the shop" },
    { "type": "url", "id": "secondary_url", "label": "Secondary link URL" }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    { "type": "point", "name": "Measurement point", "settings": [
      { "type": "select", "id": "anchor", "label": "Where it sits on the drawing",
        "info": "Two blocks on the same anchor will print two numerals on top of each other.",
        "options": [
          { "value": "collar",   "label": "Collar" },
          { "value": "shoulder", "label": "Shoulder" },
          { "value": "chest",    "label": "Chest" },
          { "value": "armhole",  "label": "Armhole" },
          { "value": "waist",    "label": "Waist" },
          { "value": "length",   "label": "Length" }
        ],
        "default": "chest" },
      { "type": "text", "id": "label", "label": "Name", "default": "Chest" },
      { "type": "text", "id": "note", "label": "Note", "info": "Optional. Keep it to a few words." }
    ]}
  ],
  "max_blocks": 6,
  "presets": [
    { "name": "Lada sizing",
      "blocks": [
        { "type": "point", "settings": { "anchor": "collar",   "label": "Collar" } },
        { "type": "point", "settings": { "anchor": "shoulder", "label": "Shoulder" } },
        { "type": "point", "settings": { "anchor": "chest",    "label": "Chest" } },
        { "type": "point", "settings": { "anchor": "armhole",  "label": "Armhole" } },
        { "type": "point", "settings": { "anchor": "waist",    "label": "Waist" } },
        { "type": "point", "settings": { "anchor": "length",   "label": "Length" } }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
